@meta
id: 04-02
title: Drill — down five, append
chapter: 04
type: drill
skills: hjkl
par: 18
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: down arrow five times
open: checklist.md

@brief
Eight times: get to the smoke test line, append ` ok`, save, quit.

Nothing to work out. Watch your right hand — if it leaves the home row,
start the rep again.

@keys
| `j` | down one line |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `:wq` | save and quit |

@hints
Five presses of `j` from the top of the file.
--
Do not look at the keyboard and do not use the arrow keys. The point of
the rep is that `j` stops being a decision.

@solution
    j j j j j A  ok <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Fourteen keystrokes, and by rep eight it should take under three seconds.

Five taps is about the limit of what is worth counting out one key at a
time. Past that you are better off giving vim a number, which is the next
lesson. This drill exists so that the short hops — two down, one up, one
right — happen without thought, because those are most of the moving you
will ever do.

@start checklist.md
# Release checklist

- [ ] run the migrations
- [ ] set the feature flags
- [ ] warm the product cache
- [ ] smoke test the checkout
- [ ] tell support

@expected checklist.md
# Release checklist

- [ ] run the migrations
- [ ] set the feature flags
- [ ] warm the product cache
- [ ] smoke test the checkout ok
- [ ] tell support
